[Line "9.g4 Be6 10.Nxe6"]
[Section "9.g4 and other set-ups"]
[Summary "White stops ...d5 with the g-pawn and takes the bishop on e6. Black gets the f-file and control of d5 in return for doubled pawns, and plays ...Ne5, ...Qc8 and ...Nc4."]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. f3 O-O 8. Qd2 Nc6 9. g4 { [%cal
Rg4g5,Rh2h4] The third main move. The pawn discourages ...d5, because g5 would drive away the knight
that supports it, and it prepares h4-h5 without spending time on Bc4. What it gives up is the f3-pawn's
stability and the option of answering ...Be6 with a bishop already on b3. } 9... Be6 { [%csl Yd5,Yc4]
The established reply, recommended by Jones and by Giri. The bishop takes the diagonal White left
empty, covers d5 and c4, and makes ...d5 a threat again. Black does not mind Nxe6: the doubled pawns
guard d5 and f5, and the f-file opens against f3. }
( 9... Nxd4 { A move order that avoids Nxe6: after 10.Bxd4 Be6 11.O-O-O Qa5 Black is in the next line. } )
10. Nxe6 { The most popular move. White takes the bishop pair and a long-term target on e6. }
10... fxe6 11. O-O-O Ne5 { [%cal Ge5f3,Ge5g4,Ge5c4] The knight hits f3 and g4 at once and forces the
bishop to a passive square. } 12. Be2 Qc8 { [%cal Gc8c4,Ge5c4] The queen supports ...Nc4, keeps an eye on
g4 and leaves d8 free. The natural 12...Rc8 is less accurate, because 13.Nb5 hits a7 and d6 while the
rook is in the queen's way. } 13. h4 Nfd7 { [%cal Gf8f3,Gd7b6] Opening the f-file and the bishop's
diagonal together. The f3-pawn is now attacked twice, and the knight is ready to recapture on e5 or go
to b6 and c4. } 14. h5
( 14. f4 { Chasing the knight where it wants to go. } 14... Nc4 15. Bxc4 Qxc4 { Black has exchanged the
bishop that held White's position together, and the pawns on e4 and g4 are loose. } )
14... Nc4 15. Bxc4 Qxc4 { **Verdict:** balanced, with the unusual structure Jones warns about. White has
the h-file and the bishop pair is gone; Black has the queen on c4, the c- and f-files, and the knight
coming to e5. The doubled e-pawns are a strength while the queens are on, since nothing can settle on
d5 or f5. Black's plan is ...Rac8, ...Ne5 and ...b5; if White opens the h-file, ...hxg6 keeps the rook
on f8 for the defence. Practice slightly favours Black. } *

[Line "9.g4 Be6 10.0-0-0 and 10.h4"]
[Section "9.g4 and other set-ups"]
[Summary "White keeps the knight and castles. Black exchanges on d4, plays ...Qa5 and ...Rfc8 with a sacrifice on c3 in the air, and the main line ends in an equal ending."]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. f3 O-O 8. Qd2 Nc6 9. g4 Be6 10. O-O-O {
White ignores the bishop and completes development. }
( 10. h4 { Attacking before castling leaves the centre unguarded. } 10... d5 { The break White's ninth
move was meant to prevent works after all, because the bishop on e6 holds d5. } 11. g5 Nh5 { The knight
blocks the h-pawn and the centre is about to open with White's king still on e1. Black has scored very
well here. } )
10... Nxd4 { Before White can play Nxe6 under better circumstances. } 11. Bxd4 Qa5 { [%cal
Gf8c8,Ga5a2,Gb7b5] The set-up of the 9.0-0-0 Nxd4 lines, with the difference that White has spent a move
on g4. } 12. Kb1 { Necessary sooner or later: 12.h4 would lose a pawn to 12...Bxa2. 12.a3 comes to the
same thing after 12...Rfc8 13.Kb1. } 12... Rfc8 { [%cal Gc8c3] Threatening the sacrifice on c3. } 13. a3 {
[%csl Ya2] Taking the sting out of it: with no pawn on a2 the queen has nothing to capture after the rook
is given. }
( 13. h4? { White cannot ignore the threat. } 13... Rxc3 { [%cal Ga5a2] } 14. Qxc3 Qxa2+ 15. Kc1 { The
king has been driven into the open, Black already has a pawn for the exchange, and the bishops and the
other rook join in. Black has won most of the games from here. } )
13... Rab8 { [%cal Gb7b5,Gb5b4] Now the pawn on a3 is a target for the b-pawn. } 14. h4 b5 15. Nd5 { The
standard reply to ...b5: with the king on b1 the queen exchange is not check, so White can choose how to
recapture. }
( 15. Bxf6 Bxf6 16. Nd5 Qxd2 17. Nxf6+ Kg7 { The in-between move. } 18. Rxd2 Kxf6 { The king is perfectly
placed in the centre of an ending, and Black has the c-file and the better bishop. } )
15... Qxd2 16. Nxf6+
( 16. Rxd2 Bxd5 { The bishop takes, keeping the knight for the dark squares. } 17. exd5 a5 { Black is
comfortable: the white bishops have nothing to aim at and the queenside pawns are rolling. } )
16... Bxf6 17. Rxd2 Bxd4 18. Rxd4 Rc5 { **Verdict:** an equal ending in which Black still has to be
careful. Material and structure are level and Black has the c-file, but White has more space on the
kingside and has won more than his share of the games, usually by pushing the h-pawn. Black should
centralise the king, keep a rook on the fifth rank and play ...a5 and ...b4 only when it creates a
target. } *

[Line "Dragadorf and the early ...h5"]
[Section "9.g4 and other set-ups"]
[Summary "An alternative to the whole main-line complex: ...a6 and ...b5 with the king left in the centre, and ...h5 to stop g4 and Bh6 before they happen."]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. f3 a6 { [%cal Gb7b5,Gc8b7,Gb8d7] The
Dragadorf: Dragon bishop, Najdorf queenside. Black does not castle and does not put the knight on c6.
With no king on g8 the h-file attack has no target, and the b-pawn comes to b5 before White has decided
where his own king is going. } 8. Qd2 h5 { [%csl Yg4,Yh5] The modern refinement, and the version Giri
gives in his bonus chapter. The pawn stops g4 for good, and Bh6 loses most of its point while there
is no king on g8 to attack. The same idea can be played a move earlier with the knight
on c6 (6...Nc6 7.f3 h5), which Ward covers under the same heading. }
( 8... Nbd7 { The original Dragadorf set-up, keeping the h-pawn at home: after 9.O-O-O b5 10.g4 Bb7 11.h4
Black usually has to play ...h5 or ...h6 anyway. It has scored well in practice, and it is the choice
for a player who wants to keep ...h5 in reserve. } )
9. O-O-O { The natural move, and the one that worries Black least. }
( 9. Bc4 { Aiming at f7 and e6 before Black is developed. } 9... Nbd7 10. O-O-O b5 11. Bb3 Bb7 { The
standard set-up: ...Rc8, ...Nc5 or ...Ne5 and ...Qc7 follow, and the king decides late whether to castle
short or stay on e8. Chances are about level. } )
( 9. Be2 { The positional treatment, and the most uncomfortable one in practice. White castles short,
where the pawn on h5 is a weakness in front of Black's king instead of a defender, and plays in the
centre. } 9... Nbd7 { Black develops in the same way, but has to accept a slightly worse game. } )
9... b5 10. Kb1 Bb7 { **Verdict:** a playable system with its own character. Black has avoided the
h-file attack, the Bh6 exchange and nearly all the theory of this chapter; the knight goes to d7 and
then c5 or b6, the rook to c8, and ...b4 or ...Qa5 starts the attack. The price is that ...d5 is hard to
arrange and that the king has no entirely safe home. Black's results against 9.O-O-O are good; the test
is the quiet 9.Be2. It is worth having as a surprise weapon and as a way of understanding why, in the
main lines, Black so often wishes the h-pawn were on h5 before White's reached h4. } *
